{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is satisfied, in both implementation and tests.**\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Orchestration.** `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs) is the central implementation. Every phase appends to one `errors` vector and none returns early. The phases are workspace config, book source, definitions, evidence files, coverage, reference discovery, reference validation, citation review and Rust item coverage.\n  - Only filesystem failures on the root itself, such as `canonicalize`, abort the run. Those are not failed checks.\n  - Each message is a `validation_error` string that starts with the file or source location. This is the \"each as a validation error\" part.\n- **Malformed `habeas.toml`.** The `load_workspace_evidence` error is pushed to `errors` and the run continues.\n  - `src/main.rs` `main` deliberately exempts `status` and `info` from the early `load_workspace_evidence(...)?`, so those commands still render the full report. The comment cites `ci.errors`.\n- **Malformed `book.toml`.** `book_source_dir` records one error for each failure and falls back to `md`, so definition collection and the later checks still run.\n  - The failures covered are a read error, invalid TOML, `book` not a table, `src` not a string, and `src` escaping the repository.\n- **Malformed evidence file.** `validate_evidence_file` reports a read failure, invalid JSON or a non-object as a single error and returns.\n  - This is the \"MAY report only the first problem\" allowance.\n  - A well-formed record reports every failed field check: the text diff, determination, rationale, references digest, dependencies and each input.\n- **Definitions.** `collect_definitions` pushes an error and continues for a bad name, an unclosed fence, a bad option, empty text, a duplicate, and an example with no requirement reference.\n- **Directory walking.** `files_with_extension` records each list, entry and file-type failure and keeps walking.\n- **Term dependencies.** `close_dependencies` and `validate_dependencies` (src/terms.rs) report every unresolvable term rather than stopping at the first.\n- **Other checks.** `validate_references`, `citations::validate`, `validate_review`, `rust_coverage::validate_rust_functions` and `collect_references_with_progress` all push errors and `continue`.\n  - A Rust file that fails to parse yields one error, and other files and items are still checked.\n  - These helpers carry no `{impl}` for `ci.errors`, but they are the per-check logic whose reported conditions other requirements define. The accumulate-and-continue convention is carried by the marked orchestrator and the marked helpers.\n- **Marking.** No behavior outside this requirement was found in the marked code. `DefinitionKind::noun` is marked because it supplies the noun used in messages; this is marginal but harmless.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` covers several cases.\n  - Several unresolvable terms plus a bad input definition are all reported, and another record's \"missing\" error still appears.\n  - An invalid `habeas.toml` (version 2), an invalid `book.toml`, a malformed evidence file and an unknown `{impl}` reference are all reported in one run.\n  - So the later checks demonstrably still run.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` checks that many different failures are collected in one run: orphan, malformed JSON, missing, text changed, false determination, and several input faults.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` shows that a broken JSON file, a broken Rust file, an uncited function and a binary file are all reported together.\n  - It also checks that the discovery-only errors exclude the uncited function.\n- `status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring` (tests/commands.rs) checks end to end that `status` and `info` succeed with an invalid `habeas.toml`.\n  - They report that finding together with the malformed evidence file.\n  - A malformed nearest `habeas.toml` is covered as well.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps)**\n\n- With an invalid `habeas.toml`, per-input path-policy checks repeat the configuration error for each input. Those checks depend on the file's allow/disallow contents, so this fits \"checks that do not depend on its contents\".\n- The tests do not exercise every `book.toml` sub-failure, such as `src` not a string. The code path is simple and uniform, so this is not a material coverage gap.",
  "references": "73d3cce44ea4f31abae83f98f96c05ebc5a843fdf5b71f871ecf557755cae031",
